#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115065 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "c0c08150-98f9-5677-a7a8-390cffc0c5b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4511" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) =0$\n\n- The system ![](images/8d/8dad0364549b8b3ea4a67136c845a979c1865ba0cc0ba8f673e247d8f26f4bf0.svg) has exactly one solution\n\n- $A^{-1}$ does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](images/98/98b93ad401053c6e2be3d5899202b6de802d0b540406797a5285360ca23b6148.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115066 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8c1e5afa-0afa-56ee-bbfa-4e84c64093ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4512" :question/problem "Let ![](images/2d/2de33a648364672e24ed14d9ceb606eeec07da3dc5e8ab1bdf4c00440f2229ac.svg) and ![](images/b3/b3ca8db3f26fed706e2786f2c7a09b39800bb249f72565039d7fe6493a813507.svg) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](images/3a/3a82d0f5d2e89d80c3e5f541e205d3052264d967db9736d86de3f52418319a29.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115067 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/id #uuid "cb19dcff-bb9d-55dc-941b-48153ca62e7a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4513" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](images/60/60966175c795cda23b462c4dc57bb694acbd0ec5ce3ffda29cb20fba41a86d60.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06497266-004d-59be-b434-27ee213d4da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259930} {:answer/id #uuid "15cd33db-73db-535d-b0c9-819460e83bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260065} {:answer/id #uuid "72dfc762-af03-5019-bea0-632e59016da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260879} {:answer/id #uuid "768f2492-46d1-5ce0-b1f2-60b8db25ce27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260911} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c267f6-962c-5b49-b551-f8a9d978a396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06497266-004d-59be-b434-27ee213d4da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259930} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1805e23-6f52-5992-81f4-50acbfc71da5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "012090a8-b5e2-5e56-87ba-9a6b486504ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-127918" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-11 \\\\ y+7x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The coefficient matrix is not invertible\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero\n- The lines $x+2y=-11$ and $y+7x=4$ intersect at infinitely many  points" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236e5192-a40c-577c-b850-ee910be00f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412410} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd3908a-3f72-5df0-977b-949624bbea37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412411} {:answer/id #uuid "ad20df4f-b7fd-5955-aa00-9373ab0ec236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412413} {:answer/id #uuid "bffc8d55-ee5e-5f8f-9215-8f1f05d7db3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412414} {:answer/id #uuid "fa69be2d-b4be-5af7-a14e-91a403fff329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ is not invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "236e5192-a40c-577c-b850-ee910be00f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412410} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcbfa28f-c9f4-5861-a817-67e808342830" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12b35a91-483f-55c8-a96f-b8126a049cff" :question/math-academy-id "q-128146" :question/problem "Consider the lines $x+y=3$ and $x-ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at exactly one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $x+y=3$ and $x-ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nFinally, notice that if $k$ can't be equal to $-1,$ since  $det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -(-1)\\end{aligned}]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*f the lines intersect at exactly one point, then $det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c21c6d-1733-55e6-ae62-ff9455761bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297633} {:answer/id #uuid "39ec9bc9-bb09-5cab-b8c8-16ed6e2d2ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297779} {:answer/id #uuid "9dffde52-21e5-5601-b349-6c8d7ff77873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298244} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4e1a09-9e67-57f1-8045-c8a0276fa706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298439} {:answer/id #uuid "dd29ba58-48e3-5699-abf9-17ba99e9924b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39ec9bc9-bb09-5cab-b8c8-16ed6e2d2ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297779} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf059d7e-0a61-50de-b3d7-bd32d0f212a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "947396a7-1dd0-5962-800b-c42f03f930a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-32091" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+\\sqrt{7}y=0.3 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{0.1}x+3y=\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The lines $3x+\\sqrt{7}y=0.3$ and $-2\\sqrt{0.1}x+3y=\\sqrt{7}$ intersect exactly at one point\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $0$\n- The coefficient matrix is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023017fb-407e-5bef-96fe-5ca4f9043072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412408} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7b32a8-3cec-559f-a17c-bf7b28c6fbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412409} {:answer/id #uuid "578d705a-4c53-5798-b6ab-04cb9c9c2712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412412} {:answer/id #uuid "cb491f25-cc92-5958-9889-8d4238a0075e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412415} {:answer/id #uuid "de2cfeee-36d6-5198-8124-8b5f27baa495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7b32a8-3cec-559f-a17c-bf7b28c6fbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412409} :answer-field/id #uuid "6af571c7-96ee-5d86-b8e5-c8021a9767c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79c14c2f-568d-5ff3-b628-6c8dd8d0639f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32169" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The lines $5x-4y=0$ and $x+2y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist\n- $Ax=0$ has no solution" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of  the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1891a184-adbe-5f7c-b5e9-88a6e9bf190d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327146} {:answer/id #uuid "36fc7ba9-3e3d-5355-b5a4-955b062950a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327412} {:answer/id #uuid "5195185b-66e6-5f1a-9118-15cd44f5a8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327643} {:answer/id #uuid "531028f9-ce96-5ffe-a8a9-62fff3ebb31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327655} {:answer/id #uuid "d67f7b2c-c5cb-5679-b7d3-c4437cfebd83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36fc7ba9-3e3d-5355-b5a4-955b062950a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327412} :answer-field/id #uuid "221c4059-f483-5fb5-989e-d4a381f46cf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5470569-5538-5c85-aec6-dfcb3a6e366a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47652" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solution\n- The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c11703-60ea-5db3-9b13-2f7a3afa17e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327246} {:answer/id #uuid "59279d06-cd25-53a1-aa91-064cd0ecbfae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327715} {:answer/id #uuid "7908c538-c70e-5c31-9c05-17320151f0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327970} {:answer/id #uuid "a57f35dc-acd0-540b-885e-0169773bdca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328363} {:answer/id #uuid "e24ee5e3-a78b-5737-9ac6-0fa569033dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23c11703-60ea-5db3-9b13-2f7a3afa17e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327246} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d0f012-54f0-5a30-83de-7d7732f83cec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffbb2a4c-595d-59cc-bffd-a39a18b105b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-47657" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the unique solution of $Ax=0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements:\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $det(A)\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $A$ is invertible .*$\\,✓$"}]}
